      "version": "0.5.0",
      "date": "2026-10-18T05:07:31Z",
      "latest": true,
      "notesHtml": "<ul><li><strong>Before / after: did your release make it slower?</strong> Every report of a request you've run before now compares with your reference run (or the previous one) in one plain sentence — <em>&quot;After is 18% slower&quot;</em>, or <em>&quot;About the same&quot;</em> — with every measure marked better, worse, or about the same, and both runs on the same charts. Small run-to-run differences don't count, and a comparison with different load is called unfair instead of judged.</li><li><strong>Compare any runs, side by side.</strong> <em>Add to compare</em> on any report collects runs in a tray at the bottom of the page — different APIs, different days. <em>Compare</em> opens them together, with a tab for each run's full report.</li><li><strong>Download for CI.</strong> On <em>Run collection</em>, one click gives you a zip for your repository: the collection (never secrets), and a ready GitHub Actions pipeline. A new guide walks you through it: apitestcenter.com/docs/ci-walkthrough.html</li><li><strong>Fail the build when an API gets slower.</strong> The command line (<code>npx apitestcenter</code>) gains <code>--baseline</code>, <code>--max-slower</code>, and <code>--save-baseline</code>, with the comparison as its own JUnit test case.</li><li><strong>Better reports.</strong> The HTML export has a Before / after section, lays out cards without gaps, and keeps chart labels readable; the response-time distribution fits its card; words no longer break in the middle.</li><li><strong>About</strong> now links to the user guide.</li></ul>\n<p>Already on 0.3.0 or later? The app updates itself (installer version) — just restart when it asks.</p>",
